{%- # theme-check-disable -%}
{%- comment -%} theme-check parses every .liquid file as HTML, where <link>
    is a void element and <channel> does not exist - so it reports
    "Attempting to close HtmlElement 'link' before 'channel' was closed"
    on valid RSS 2.0. This file is XML, not HTML. Verified against the live
    URL with a strict DOMParser: 66 items, parses true, 0 unescaped
    ampersands. The linter is wrong about a file that is not HTML.
    Whitespace-stripping markers are REQUIRED on both tags: the XML declaration
    must be the first byte of the response, and a stray newline ahead of it
    makes a strict parser reject the whole document. {%- endcomment -%}
{%- layout none -%}
{%- comment -%}
================================================================================
  PINTEREST PRODUCT FEED (RSS 2.0, Google Merchant g: namespace)
  Jeff Combs, Atlas Digital Design · 4 September 2026
  templates/collection.pinterest-feed.liquid
  Live at:  /collections/all?view=pinterest-feed
================================================================================

  WHY THIS EXISTS
  The native Shopify->Pinterest integration emits one catalog row per VARIANT and
  appends ?variant=<id> to every link. Verified 4 Sep: that made one plan render
  at more than twice its entry price depending on which pin was clicked, and only
  about one catalog row in five carried the entry price. It also sends body_html as the
  description, which now opens with the modification CTA, and it emits no
  item_group_id and no UTMs.

  None of that is fixable inside the native app. This template replaces it:
  one row per plan, the entry price, a clean link, the SEO description.

  PHASE CONTROL — read this before pasting
  ----------------------------------------
  PHASE A (default below): one plan, and a DELIBERATELY NON-COLLIDING id.
     Proves the feed parses and the fields populate, with zero risk to the
     existing 322 catalog items because it targets none of their ids.
  PHASE B: set test_id_suffix to blank. Same single plan, now using the REAL
     base-tier variant id. Force ingestion and check within 24h, before the
     Shopify app's next sync, whether the item UPDATED or DUPLICATED.
  PHASE C: set test_handles to blank to expose all 66 plans. Expect EXACTLY 66
     (verified 5 Sept: 66 active Plan Sets, 45 Study Sets excluded by type).
     Then let BOTH sources overlap for one sync cycle and confirm the Liquid rows
     won before severing Shopify. Corrected 5 Sept: the earlier instruction said
     sever immediately. That is backwards. If Shopify wins the race we are merely
     back to the status quo and can retry; if we sever first and the feed has a
     problem at 66-scale, the catalog has no source at all. Overlap, verify, cut.
     SEVER MEANS: stop the Shopify app's CATALOG feed only. Do NOT uninstall the
     Pinterest sales channel app without first confirming what else it carries -
     the domain claim and the Pinterest tag are worth more than this feed.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- comment -%} ============ PHASE SETTINGS ============ {%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign test_handles = '' -%}
{%- assign test_id_suffix = '' -%}
{%- assign shop_url = shop.url -%}

{%- capture utm -%}utm_source=pinterest&amp;utm_medium=product_pin&amp;utm_campaign=catalog{%- endcapture -%}
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<rss version="2.0" xmlns:g="http://base.google.com/ns/1.0">
<channel>
<title>{{ shop.name | escape }}</title>
<link>{{ shop_url }}</link>
<description>Designer-direct coastal, elevated and garage/ADU house plans.</description>
{%- comment -%} paginate is REQUIRED: a bare Liquid for loop caps at 50 iterations,
    so without it the feed would silently ship 50 of 66 plans. Shopify allows a
    page_size of 1-250, and Pinterest fetches the bare URL (page 1 only), so this
    holds the whole catalog on one page TODAY. CEILING: if the catalog ever passes
    250 Plan Sets, this feed truncates without erroring. Verified 5 Sept: 66 active
    Plan Sets, 45 Study Sets excluded by the product.type filter above. {%- endcomment -%}
{%- paginate collections.all.products by 250 -%}
{%- for product in collections.all.products -%}

  {%- comment -%} Plan Sets only. Study sets stay off Pinterest by decision:
      every one reuses its plan's own render, so publishing them would add
      duplicate-image items competing with the plan pins. {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- unless product.type == 'Plan Set' -%}{%- continue -%}{%- endunless -%}
  {%- unless product.available -%}{%- continue -%}{%- endunless -%}
  {%- if test_handles != blank -%}
    {%- assign wanted = test_handles | split: ',' -%}
    {%- unless wanted contains product.handle -%}{%- continue -%}{%- endunless -%}
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%} The BASE tier is variant #1 (PDF Set / Pier). Its id is what the
      native integration already uses for that row, so reusing it is what lets
      Pinterest update the existing item instead of minting a new pin with no
      history. {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign v = product.variants.first -%}

  {%- comment -%} .value on every one. A metafield renders its value when output,
      but a comparison against the OBJECT is always false - `adu == true` would be
      silently wrong forever and every plan would file as a House Plan. {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- comment -%} Namespace insurance. A historical typo split this data: the
      CORRECT `plan_specs` namespace holds bedrooms_filter / bathrooms_filter,
      while the misspelled `plans_specs` holds differently-named keys, bedrooms
      and bathrooms. Verified 4 Sept across all 66 active plan sets: every one
      has BOTH, so this fallback never fires today. It is here so a plan created
      later with only the typo namespace still renders rather than shipping a
      blank spec to Pinterest. Note the key names differ between namespaces —
      chaining to plans_specs.bedrooms_FILTER would be a no-op. {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign beds   = product.metafields.plan_specs.bedrooms_filter.value  | default: product.metafields.plans_specs.bedrooms.value -%}
  {%- assign baths  = product.metafields.plan_specs.bathrooms_filter.value | default: product.metafields.plans_specs.bathrooms.value -%}
  {%- assign band   = product.metafields.plan_specs.sqft_range.value -%}

  {%- comment -%} A studio has 0 bedrooms, which is CORRECT data but renders as
      "0 Bedroom" in product_type and "0BR" in custom_label_2. Verified 5 Sept:
      one studio garage plan is the only one this hits. Compare
      against both 0 and '0' - a metafield .value may arrive as either. {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if beds == 0 or beds == '0' -%}
    {%- assign bed_node  = 'Studio' -%}
    {%- assign bed_short = 'Studio' -%}
  {%- else -%}
    {%- capture bed_node  -%}{{ beds }} Bedroom{%- endcapture -%}
    {%- capture bed_short -%}{{ beds }}BR{%- endcapture -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- assign elev   = product.metafields.plan_specs.elevation.value -%}
  {%- assign found  = product.metafields.plan_specs.foundation_type.value -%}
  {%- assign ptype  = product.metafields.plan_specs.plan_type.value -%}
  {%- assign adu    = product.metafields.plan_specs.adu_included.value -%}
  {%- assign studyv = product.metafields.custom.study_set_variant_id.value -%}

  {%- comment -%} Description: the SEO metafield, NOT body_html. This is the whole
      point — body_html now opens with "Would you like to customize this plan?"
      and that is the first thing a person reads on an organic pin closeup. {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign desc = product.metafields.global.description_tag.value | default: product.description | strip_html -%}

  {%- comment -%} product_type: a real hierarchy. Pinterest accepts 5 levels and
      states deeper hierarchies help checkout propensity. {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- comment -%} adu_included is present on all but one plan, which
      lacks it. That plan IS a house plan, so a missing value falling to the else
      branch is already correct. Fails safe. {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if adu == true -%}
    {%- assign l1 = 'Garage &amp; ADU Plans' -%}
  {%- else -%}
    {%- assign l1 = 'House Plans' -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if elev == 'Elevated' -%}{%- assign l3 = found -%}{%- else -%}{%- assign l3 = found | default: 'Slab' -%}{%- endif -%}

<item>
  <g:id>{{ v.id }}{{ test_id_suffix }}</g:id>
  <g:item_group_id>{{ product.handle }}</g:item_group_id>
  <title>{{ product.title | strip_html | escape | truncate: 100, "" }}</title>
  <g:description>{{ desc | escape | truncate: 500, "" }}</g:description>

  {%- comment -%} THE FIX. Canonical product URL, no ?variant=, so every pin lands
      on the entry price. UTMs so Pinterest stops arriving as unattributable
      referral traffic. {%- endcomment -%}
  <link>{{ shop_url }}/products/{{ product.handle }}?{{ utm }}&amp;utm_content={{ product.handle }}</link>
  {%- comment -%} image_url returns a protocol-relative //cdn.shopify.com URL. The
      same thing broke the modification email in August - force the scheme. {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign img = product.featured_image | image_url: width: 1000 -%}
  {%- if img contains 'http' -%}{%- assign img_abs = img -%}{%- else -%}{%- assign img_abs = img | prepend: 'https:' -%}{%- endif -%}
  {%- comment -%} `| escape` is not optional. image_url appends its size parameter
      with a RAW ampersand (…jpg?v=1771195111&width=1000), which XML reads as a
      broken entity reference and rejects the whole document. Caught 4 Sept by
      running the live feed through a strict DOMParser — a browser renders it
      fine, so this is invisible without an actual XML parse. {%- endcomment -%}
  <g:image_link>{{ img_abs | escape }}</g:image_link>

  <g:availability>in stock</g:availability>
  <g:condition>new</g:condition>
  <g:brand>{{ shop.name | escape }}</g:brand>
  {%- comment -%} VERIFIED 4 Sept from the store's Google Merchant Center account: the Shopify integration sends
      google_product_category = 8022, and GMC approves every item on it. This is
      the known-good numeric ID, not a guess. Pinterest accepts the numeric form.
      (Label not resolved from here — Google's taxonomy file is blocked by egress.
      It is readable in the GMC product editor if the human-readable path is ever
      needed.) {%- endcomment -%}
  <g:google_product_category>8022</g:google_product_category>
  <g:product_type>{{ l1 }} &gt; Coastal &gt; {{ l3 | escape }} &gt; {{ bed_node }} &gt; {{ band | escape }}</g:product_type>

  {%- comment -%} List price and sale price kept the right way round: g:price is
      compare-at, g:sale_price is what they pay. Verified the strikethrough and
      "16% off" badge already render correctly from these. {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if v.compare_at_price > v.price -%}
  <g:price>{{ v.compare_at_price | money_without_currency | remove: ',' }} USD</g:price>
  <g:sale_price>{{ v.price | money_without_currency | remove: ',' }} USD</g:sale_price>
  {%- else -%}
  <g:price>{{ v.price | money_without_currency | remove: ',' }} USD</g:price>
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%} REVENUE TIER. custom_label_0 used to repeat the square-footage
      band, which product_type already carries at level 5, so the slot was free.
      Tier A is the hand-kept list of plans the order ledger shows actually sell;
      tier B is the right segment but unproven; tier C browses and does not buy.
      Used to decide where fresh creative and ad spend go.
      NOTE: hand-maintained. Revisit against the order ledger, not from memory.
      The handles below are placeholders; the store's real list is not published. {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign tier_a = 'example-plan-one,example-plan-two,example-plan-three' | split: ',' -%}
  {%- if tier_a contains product.handle -%}
    {%- assign tier = 'tier-a-proven' -%}
  {%- elsif elev == 'Elevated' or ptype == 'Garage + ADU Plan' -%}
    {%- assign tier = 'tier-b-segment' -%}
  {%- else -%}
    {%- assign tier = 'tier-c-slow' -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  <g:custom_label_0>{{ tier }}</g:custom_label_0>
  <g:custom_label_1>{{ elev | default: 'Standard' | escape }}</g:custom_label_1>
  <g:custom_label_2>{{ bed_short }}-{{ baths }}BA</g:custom_label_2>
  <g:custom_label_3>{{ ptype | escape }}</g:custom_label_3>
  <g:custom_label_4>{% if studyv != blank %}study-set-yes{% else %}study-set-no{% endif %}</g:custom_label_4>
</item>
{%- endfor -%}
{%- endpaginate -%}
</channel>
</rss>